    "result_text": "The full suite is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nThe `Owner` model already carried `customerCode` and `registrationDate`; the only missing behaviour was the audit side-effect on successful create.\n\n**Change (2 files, no method bodies touched):**\n- Added `model/OwnerAuditListener.java` \u2014 a small, single-method JPA `@PostPersist` listener that writes one line to the `AUDIT` logger with the owner's `id`, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate`.\n- Registered it on `Owner` with a class-level `@EntityListeners(OwnerAuditListener.class)` annotation (using the already-present `jakarta.persistence.*` import).\n\n**Why this keeps structural impact low:** `@PostPersist` fires only on insert (creates, not updates), and only after the id is generated and `@PrePersist` has finalised `registrationDate` \u2014 so all three values are present and match what `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns. I deliberately avoided adding a line to the large, high-complexity `addOwner` controller method or into the large `Owner` class body; the new logic lives in a fresh class with zero surrounding complexity (`WMC_other \u2248 0`), and the `Owner` edit changes no function, only a declaration annotation.\n\nVerified: `AUDIT - owner created id=11 customerCode=TOW-SUR-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-01`, and `BUILD SUCCESS` with all tests passing.",
